WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.896 --> 00:05.896 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference January 29,

00:05.896 --> 00:07.116 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:07.116 --> 00:07.526 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:07.626 --> 00:10.196 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:10.196 --> 00:13.446 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:13.446 --> 00:16.356 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:17.356 --> 00:20.876 align:middle
The economy is strong-is
strong overall

00:20.986 --> 00:24.386 align:middle
and has made significant
progress toward our goals

00:24.426 --> 00:25.706 align:middle
over the past two years.

00:26.916 --> 00:28.516 align:middle
Labor market conditions
have cooled

00:28.516 --> 00:31.636 align:middle
from their formerly overheated
state and remain solid.

00:33.006 --> 00:34.726 align:middle
Inflation has moved much closer

00:34.726 --> 00:36.556 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal,

00:37.026 --> 00:38.786 align:middle
though it remains
somewhat elevated.

00:40.366 --> 00:41.466 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:41.466 --> 00:43.476 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:43.476 --> 00:47.086 align:middle
to leave our policy interest
rate unchanged and to continue

00:47.086 --> 00:48.786 align:middle
to reduce our securities
holdings.

00:50.216 --> 00:52.176 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about monetary policy

00:52.176 --> 00:54.726 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

00:56.696 --> 01:00.176 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
economic activity has continued

01:00.176 --> 01:01.816 align:middle
to expand at a solid pace.

01:02.746 --> 01:07.876 align:middle
For 2024 as a whole, GDP looks
to have risen above 2 percent,

01:08.446 --> 01:10.346 align:middle
bolstered by resilient
consumer spending.

01:12.096 --> 01:14.416 align:middle
Investment in equipment
and intangibles appears

01:14.416 --> 01:15.936 align:middle
to have slowed in
the fourth quarter

01:16.476 --> 01:18.316 align:middle
but was strong for
the year overall.

01:19.526 --> 01:21.516 align:middle
Following weakness in
the middle of last year,

01:21.516 --> 01:24.436 align:middle
activity in the housing sector
seems to have stabilized.

01:25.216 --> 01:28.526 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions remain solid.

01:29.566 --> 01:32.886 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 170,000 per month

01:32.886 --> 01:34.066 align:middle
over the past three months.

01:34.926 --> 01:38.216 align:middle
Following earlier increases, the
unemployment rate has stabilized

01:38.216 --> 01:41.586 align:middle
since the middle of last
year and, at 4.1 percent

01:41.586 --> 01:43.016 align:middle
in December, remains low.

01:44.816 --> 01:47.396 align:middle
Nominal wage growth has
eased over the past year,

01:48.266 --> 01:50.326 align:middle
and the jobs-to-workers
gap has narrowed.

01:51.606 --> 01:53.976 align:middle
Overall, a wide set
of indicators suggests

01:53.976 --> 01:56.846 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are broadly in balance.

01:58.056 --> 02:00.086 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:00.086 --> 02:01.976 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

02:04.186 --> 02:05.696 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly

02:05.816 --> 02:07.056 align:middle
over the past two years

02:07.546 --> 02:09.396 align:middle
but remains somewhat
elevated relative

02:09.396 --> 02:11.076 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:12.106 --> 02:14.256 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:14.256 --> 02:15.396 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:15.396 --> 02:18.226 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.6 percent

02:18.756 --> 02:21.296 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in December and that,

02:21.296 --> 02:23.776 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:24.126 --> 02:26.796 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent.

02:27.996 --> 02:30.386 align:middle
Longer-term inflation
expectations appear

02:30.386 --> 02:34.146 align:middle
to remain well anchored, as
reflected in a broad range

02:34.146 --> 02:37.006 align:middle
of surveys of households,
businesses, and forecasters,

02:37.596 --> 02:39.526 align:middle
as well as measures
from financial markets.

02:40.786 --> 02:43.806 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

02:43.956 --> 02:46.846 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

02:46.846 --> 02:47.936 align:middle
for the American people.

02:49.126 --> 02:51.176 align:middle
We see the risks to
achieving our employment

02:51.176 --> 02:53.856 align:middle
and inflation goals as
being roughly in balance,

02:54.606 --> 02:55.986 align:middle
and we are attentive
to the risks

02:56.036 --> 02:57.516 align:middle
on both sides of our mandate.

02:59.946 --> 03:01.876 align:middle
Over the course of our
three previous meetings,

03:01.876 --> 03:03.246 align:middle
we lowered our policy rate

03:03.246 --> 03:05.006 align:middle
by a full percentage
point from its peak.

03:06.076 --> 03:09.656 align:middle
That recalibration of our policy
stance was appropriate in light

03:09.656 --> 03:10.956 align:middle
of the progress on inflation

03:11.376 --> 03:13.066 align:middle
and the rebalancing
in the labor market.

03:14.456 --> 03:17.076 align:middle
With our policy stance
significantly less restrictive

03:17.076 --> 03:21.496 align:middle
than it had been and the economy
remaining strong, we do not need

03:21.496 --> 03:24.136 align:middle
to be in a hurry to
adjust our policy stance.

03:25.556 --> 03:27.096 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:27.096 --> 03:29.556 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:29.556 --> 03:32.106 align:middle
at 4¼ to 4½ percent.

03:34.136 --> 03:36.956 align:middle
We know that reducing
policy restraint too fast

03:36.956 --> 03:39.896 align:middle
or too much could hinder
progress on inflation.

03:41.036 --> 03:44.356 align:middle
At the same time, reducing
policy restraint too slowly

03:44.516 --> 03:47.596 align:middle
or too little could unduly
weaken economic activity

03:47.716 --> 03:48.336 align:middle
and employment.

03:49.826 --> 03:52.566 align:middle
In considering the extent and
timing of additional adjustments

03:52.566 --> 03:54.426 align:middle
to the target range for
the federal funds rate,

03:55.106 --> 03:57.206 align:middle
the Committee will
assess incoming data,

03:57.746 --> 04:00.126 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

04:00.946 --> 04:02.546 align:middle
We're not on any preset course.

04:03.416 --> 04:07.996 align:middle
As the economy evolves, we
will adjust our policy stance

04:07.996 --> 04:10.506 align:middle
in a manner that best
promotes our maximum-employment

04:10.506 --> 04:11.806 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

04:12.676 --> 04:14.916 align:middle
If the economy remains-economy
remains strong

04:14.916 --> 04:16.216 align:middle
and inflation does not continue

04:16.216 --> 04:18.006 align:middle
to move sustainably
toward 2 percent,

04:18.706 --> 04:20.876 align:middle
we can maintain policy
restraint for longer.

04:21.406 --> 04:24.246 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

04:24.476 --> 04:27.136 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

04:27.636 --> 04:29.336 align:middle
we can ease policy accordingly.

04:30.546 --> 04:32.806 align:middle
Policy is well positioned
to deal with the risks

04:32.876 --> 04:34.286 align:middle
and uncertainties that we face

04:34.396 --> 04:36.846 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

04:39.056 --> 04:41.506 align:middle
As we previously announced,
our five-year review,

04:41.646 --> 04:44.296 align:middle
review of our monetary policy
framework is taking place

04:44.356 --> 04:44.766 align:middle
this year.

04:45.576 --> 04:47.696 align:middle
At this meeting, the
Committee began its discussions

04:47.696 --> 04:50.896 align:middle
by reviewing the context and
outcomes of our previous review

04:51.016 --> 04:54.486 align:middle
that concluded in 2020 as
well as the experiences

04:54.486 --> 04:56.566 align:middle
of other central banks
in conducting reviews.

04:57.826 --> 04:59.696 align:middle
Our review will again
include outreach

04:59.696 --> 05:02.036 align:middle
and public events involving
a wide range of parties,

05:02.216 --> 05:04.386 align:middle
including Fed Listens
events around the country

05:04.766 --> 05:06.536 align:middle
and a research conference
in May.

05:07.676 --> 05:10.226 align:middle
Throughout this process, we
will be open to new ideas

05:10.226 --> 05:13.156 align:middle
and critical feedback, and
we will take on board lessons

05:13.156 --> 05:16.096 align:middle
of the last five years in
determining our findings.

05:17.456 --> 05:19.626 align:middle
We intend to wrap up the
review by late summer.

05:20.726 --> 05:22.766 align:middle
I would note that the
Committee's 2 percent longer-run

05:22.766 --> 05:24.576 align:middle
inflation goal will,
will be retained

05:24.736 --> 05:26.876 align:middle
and will not be a
focus of the review.

05:28.896 --> 05:31.406 align:middle
The Fed has been assigned two
goals for monetary policy:

05:31.526 --> 05:33.666 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

05:34.296 --> 05:36.716 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

05:37.266 --> 05:39.896 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

05:40.446 --> 05:43.636 align:middle
and keeping longer-run inflation
expectations well anchored.

05:44.876 --> 05:46.016 align:middle
Our success in delivering

05:46.016 --> 05:48.156 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

05:48.926 --> 05:51.846 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

05:51.846 --> 05:53.376 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

05:54.376 --> 05:56.796 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

05:57.906 --> 05:59.566 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

05:59.786 --> 06:01.716 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

06:01.716 --> 06:02.896 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

06:03.666 --> 06:04.076 align:middle
Thank you.

06:04.226 --> 06:05.956 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

06:06.816 --> 06:07.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

06:08.426 --> 06:11.046 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

06:11.046 --> 06:13.186 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Steve
Liesman from CNBC.

06:13.456 --> 06:18.286 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, all right, at
an event in Davos-or from,

06:18.346 --> 06:20.946 align:middle
to Davos, anyway-the
President said he'll demand

06:20.946 --> 06:23.106 align:middle
that interest rates
drop immediately.

06:23.796 --> 06:25.216 align:middle
So I guess I have a
three-part question.

06:25.216 --> 06:27.396 align:middle
Has the President
done this to you?

06:27.396 --> 06:28.606 align:middle
Has he made that demand?

06:29.996 --> 06:32.396 align:middle
Secondly, what is
your response to that?

06:32.396 --> 06:34.976 align:middle
And, third, what effect,
if anything-if any,

06:34.976 --> 06:37.686 align:middle
does a President making these
kind of remarks have on policy?

06:37.736 --> 06:38.086 align:middle
Thank you.

06:38.896 --> 06:39.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

06:40.016 --> 06:40.846 align:middle
Three questions.

06:42.266 --> 06:44.286 align:middle
I'm seeing, seeing it really
as one question, though.

06:44.286 --> 06:46.646 align:middle
So, I, I'm not going to
have-I'm not going to have any,

06:46.736 --> 06:48.936 align:middle
any response or comment
whatsoever on,

06:48.996 --> 06:50.066 align:middle
on what the President's said.

06:50.616 --> 06:52.016 align:middle
It's not appropriate
for me to do so.

06:52.596 --> 06:55.226 align:middle
The public should be
confident that we will continue

06:55.226 --> 06:58.776 align:middle
to do our work as we always
have-focusing on using our tools

06:58.776 --> 07:02.676 align:middle
to achieve our goals, and
really keeping our heads

07:02.676 --> 07:03.546 align:middle
down and doing our work.

07:03.546 --> 07:05.076 align:middle
And that's how we
best serve the public.

07:05.076 --> 07:05.406 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:05.986 --> 07:06.966 align:middle
Could you just comment

07:06.966 --> 07:09.436 align:middle
on whether he's physically
communicated his demand to you?

07:09.586 --> 07:09.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:09.876 --> 07:10.706 align:middle
I've had no contact.

07:10.746 --> 07:12.156 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

07:12.706 --> 07:13.396 align:middle
Thank you.

07:13.766 --> 07:14.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:14.416 --> 07:15.466 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

07:15.876 --> 07:16.746 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

07:16.746 --> 07:18.186 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

07:18.846 --> 07:20.836 align:middle
Chair Powell, you and several
of your colleagues said

07:20.836 --> 07:22.326 align:middle
around the time of
the last meeting

07:22.686 --> 07:25.566 align:middle
that your policy stance was
"meaningfully restrictive."

07:26.196 --> 07:29.566 align:middle
Given economic and financial
market developments since then,

07:30.036 --> 07:32.766 align:middle
how has your confidence
changed in an assessment

07:32.766 --> 07:36.026 align:middle
that says interest rates are
meaningfully restrictive?

07:37.336 --> 07:37.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:37.546 --> 07:40.156 align:middle
I don't think that my
assessment really has changed.

07:40.366 --> 07:42.126 align:middle
I mean, a couple of
things have happened.

07:42.126 --> 07:45.886 align:middle
We've gotten more strong data,
but we've also seen rates move

07:45.886 --> 07:48.406 align:middle
up at the long end, which
could represent a tightening

07:48.406 --> 07:49.386 align:middle
in financial conditions.

07:49.896 --> 07:52.516 align:middle
I think if we look back
over the past year or so,

07:53.186 --> 07:55.016 align:middle
we can see that policy
is restrictive.

07:55.016 --> 07:56.726 align:middle
If you look at the
effect of high rates

07:56.726 --> 07:58.586 align:middle
on interest-sensitive
spending-for example,

07:58.586 --> 08:00.746 align:middle
in housing-and if you
look at the achievement

08:00.746 --> 08:01.936 align:middle
of our goal variables,

08:01.936 --> 08:05.516 align:middle
we're seeing the economy move
toward 2 percent inflation and,

08:06.006 --> 08:09.606 align:middle
and has moved largely to
maximum employment, so.

08:09.926 --> 08:10.686 align:middle
Though we literally look

08:10.686 --> 08:13.486 align:middle
at the-at the-at movement
toward the goal variables

08:13.486 --> 08:14.396 align:middle
to make that assessment.

08:14.746 --> 08:17.436 align:middle
Now, policy is meaningfully
less restrictive

08:17.436 --> 08:19.016 align:middle
than it was before
we began to cut.

08:19.016 --> 08:20.766 align:middle
It's 100 basis points
less restrictive.

08:21.386 --> 08:24.516 align:middle
And for that reason, you know,
we're going to be focusing

08:24.516 --> 08:28.336 align:middle
on seeing real progress on
inflation or, alternatively,

08:28.336 --> 08:31.066 align:middle
some weakness in the labor
market before we-before we

08:31.066 --> 08:32.916 align:middle
consider making adjustments.

08:33.156 --> 08:33.516 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:33.516 --> 08:34.186 align:middle
If I could follow up.

08:34.446 --> 08:37.166 align:middle
Does the economy here warrant
meaningfully restrictive

08:37.166 --> 08:40.066 align:middle
interest rates, and would
you judge interest rates

08:40.066 --> 08:42.346 align:middle
to still be meaningfully
restrictive if you were

08:42.346 --> 08:43.726 align:middle
to lower them by
another ¼ point?

08:43.726 --> 08:45.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:45.426 --> 08:49.336 align:middle
So, I, I think our policy
stance is very well calibrated,

08:49.336 --> 08:51.766 align:middle
as I mentioned, to
balance the achievement

08:51.766 --> 08:53.476 align:middle
of our two-of our two goals.

08:54.026 --> 08:58.606 align:middle
We, we want to-policy to be
restrictive enough to continue

08:58.606 --> 09:00.516 align:middle
to foster further,
further progress

09:01.056 --> 09:02.926 align:middle
for our 2 percent
inflation goal.

09:02.926 --> 09:05.446 align:middle
At the same time, we don't
need to see further weakening

09:05.446 --> 09:08.786 align:middle
in the labor market to achieve
that goal, and that's kind

09:08.786 --> 09:09.546 align:middle
of what we've been getting.

09:09.546 --> 09:12.066 align:middle
The labor market has
really been broadly stable;

09:12.066 --> 09:15.116 align:middle
the unemployment rate has
been broadly stable now

09:15.116 --> 09:15.986 align:middle
for six months.

09:16.116 --> 09:17.896 align:middle
Conditions seem to be
broadly in balance.

09:18.256 --> 09:19.856 align:middle
And I would say, look
at the last couple

09:19.856 --> 09:23.486 align:middle
of inflation readings and you
see we don't-we don't overreact

09:23.606 --> 09:25.576 align:middle
to two good readings
or two bad readings.

09:25.606 --> 09:27.266 align:middle
But, nonetheless,
the last couple

09:27.266 --> 09:30.356 align:middle
of readings have suggested, you
know, more positive readings.

09:30.356 --> 09:33.506 align:middle
So I, I think we're-I think
policy's well, well positioned.

09:33.566 --> 09:35.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:35.176 --> 09:37.706 align:middle
Colby, from the New York Times.

09:38.256 --> 09:38.596 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

09:38.706 --> 09:40.576 align:middle
Colby Smith, with
the New York Times.

09:40.576 --> 09:42.296 align:middle
Chair Powell, how should
we interpret the removal

09:42.296 --> 09:43.436 align:middle
of the line from the statement

09:43.436 --> 09:46.336 align:middle
that inflation has made progress
towards the 2 percent goal?

09:46.616 --> 09:47.676 align:middle
Is that no longer the case?

09:48.126 --> 09:48.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:48.806 --> 09:52.206 align:middle
No, so let me look-if you just
look at the first paragraph,

09:52.426 --> 09:54.416 align:middle
we did a little bit of
language cleanup there.

09:54.416 --> 09:57.476 align:middle
We took out a reference to
"since earlier in the year"

09:57.896 --> 10:01.486 align:middle
as it related to the labor
market, and we just chose to,

10:01.666 --> 10:02.936 align:middle
to shorten that sentence.

10:03.266 --> 10:05.526 align:middle
Again, I, I mean, if
you look at the sort

10:05.526 --> 10:07.446 align:middle
of intermeeting data
was good, and,

10:07.446 --> 10:09.906 align:middle
and there was-there was another
inflation reading, I guess,

10:09.906 --> 10:11.096 align:middle
just before the December
meeting.

10:11.096 --> 10:13.136 align:middle
So, we've got two, two
good readings in a row

10:13.136 --> 10:14.886 align:middle
that are consistent with
2 percent inflation.

10:14.966 --> 10:17.366 align:middle
Again, we're not going
to overinterpret two good

10:17.366 --> 10:19.756 align:middle
or two bad readings,
but this was not meant

10:19.756 --> 10:22.356 align:middle
to send a signal other
than this: You know, you,

10:22.356 --> 10:25.426 align:middle
you can take away from all of
this that we remain committed

10:25.856 --> 10:29.026 align:middle
to achieving our 2 percent
inflation goal sustainably.

10:29.466 --> 10:29.916 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

10:29.916 --> 10:32.766 align:middle
And just to follow up: We've
seen inflation expectations

10:32.766 --> 10:35.966 align:middle
across a number of measures
rise sharply-which has, in part,

10:35.966 --> 10:37.476 align:middle
been linked to tariff concerns.

10:37.836 --> 10:39.446 align:middle
But there's also been
this encouraging data

10:39.446 --> 10:42.426 align:middle
that you mentioned in terms
of CPI and rent indices.

10:42.956 --> 10:45.926 align:middle
So how would you characterize
concerns about upside risks

10:46.026 --> 10:48.686 align:middle
to inflation across the
Committee, especially those tied

10:48.726 --> 10:50.866 align:middle
to policies related to
the Trump Administration?

10:51.586 --> 10:52.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:52.656 --> 10:55.306 align:middle
Well I, I'd say you, you
see expectations moving

10:55.306 --> 10:58.476 align:middle
up a little bit, at the short
end-but not at the longer run,

10:58.476 --> 10:59.486 align:middle
which [is] where
it really matters.

10:59.486 --> 11:01.756 align:middle
And those could be
related to-could be related

11:01.756 --> 11:03.716 align:middle
to what you mentioned,
some of the new policies.

11:04.376 --> 11:09.376 align:middle
I think where the Committee is
very much in the mode of waiting

11:09.376 --> 11:11.476 align:middle
to see what policies
are enacted.

11:11.476 --> 11:15.226 align:middle
We, and we, we don't know what
will happen with, with tariffs,

11:15.226 --> 11:18.056 align:middle
with immigration,
with fiscal policy,

11:18.256 --> 11:20.016 align:middle
and with regulatory policy.

11:20.656 --> 11:23.096 align:middle
We're only just beginning
to see-actually,

11:23.096 --> 11:24.386 align:middle
are not really beginning
to see much,

11:24.896 --> 11:26.056 align:middle
and I think we need to-we need

11:26.056 --> 11:29.126 align:middle
to let those policies be
articulated before we can even

11:29.126 --> 11:31.696 align:middle
begin to make a, a
plausible assessment

11:31.696 --> 11:33.476 align:middle
of what their implications
for the economy will be.

11:33.476 --> 11:35.746 align:middle
So we're going to be
watching carefully and,

11:35.746 --> 11:36.676 align:middle
and, as we always do.

11:36.676 --> 11:39.606 align:middle
This is no different than any
other set of policy changes

11:39.606 --> 11:41.266 align:middle
at the beginning of
a-of an Administration.

11:41.866 --> 11:45.146 align:middle
We'll patiently watch and
understand and, and, you know,

11:45.356 --> 11:48.966 align:middle
kind of not be in a hurry
to, to get to a place

11:48.966 --> 11:51.536 align:middle
of understanding what our
policy response should be

11:51.536 --> 11:52.656 align:middle
until we see how it plays out.

11:52.656 --> 11:54.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:54.626 --> 11:55.706 align:middle
Michael McKee.

11:56.306 --> 11:57.136 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

11:57.856 --> 12:00.176 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

12:00.586 --> 12:04.756 align:middle
You and your colleagues normally
condition future policy moves

12:04.756 --> 12:08.266 align:middle
with the phrase, "if the economy
develops as we anticipate."

12:08.846 --> 12:12.076 align:middle
Is it fair to say that
since there's a lot unknown

12:12.076 --> 12:14.866 align:middle
about what this Administration's
fiscal policies are actually

12:14.866 --> 12:17.826 align:middle
going to be that you
don't have a medium-

12:17.826 --> 12:21.256 align:middle
to long-term economic forecast
that you can have confidence in?

12:21.906 --> 12:26.366 align:middle
Or, if that's not true, can you
lay out what you think is going

12:26.366 --> 12:28.536 align:middle
to happen in the economy,
how you see it developing?

12:28.986 --> 12:29.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:29.196 --> 12:33.146 align:middle
Well, at all times-at all times,
forecasts are conditional,

12:33.146 --> 12:37.296 align:middle
at a minimum, on just a
set of expectations, and,

12:37.776 --> 12:40.616 align:middle
and they're highly
uncertain in both directions.

12:40.616 --> 12:43.036 align:middle
We, we know that
economic forecasting is,

12:43.066 --> 12:47.236 align:middle
is really difficult, beyond
just a month or two out.

12:48.006 --> 12:50.176 align:middle
So in the current situation,

12:50.216 --> 12:55.246 align:middle
there's probably some elevated
uncertainty because of, of,

12:55.246 --> 12:57.956 align:middle
you know, significant policy
shifts in those four areas

12:57.956 --> 13:00.846 align:middle
that I mentioned: tariffs,
tariffs, immigration,

13:01.326 --> 13:03.846 align:middle
fiscal policy, and,
and regulatory policy.

13:04.066 --> 13:06.156 align:middle
So there's probably some
additional uncertainty,

13:06.156 --> 13:08.126 align:middle
but that should be passing;
we should go through that.

13:08.556 --> 13:10.856 align:middle
And then we'll be back to the
regular amount of uncertain.

13:11.206 --> 13:13.996 align:middle
You know, what, what forecasters
are doing-not just us,

13:13.996 --> 13:17.156 align:middle
but everybody's doing-is
they've got sort of just a set

13:17.156 --> 13:19.596 align:middle
of assumptions about what might
happen, but they're really kind

13:19.596 --> 13:22.416 align:middle
of in the nature of a
placeholder, and-meaning,

13:22.506 --> 13:25.016 align:middle
you know, plausible, could be,

13:25.016 --> 13:26.926 align:middle
but honestly you
wouldn't stand behind it

13:26.926 --> 13:28.896 align:middle
because you just don't know,
and so you're just-you're just

13:28.896 --> 13:32.236 align:middle
on hold, waiting to
see what comes down.

13:32.236 --> 13:33.966 align:middle
You know, it's, it's
a very large economy,

13:33.966 --> 13:37.306 align:middle
and policies affect it at the
margin, but we'll, you know,

13:37.306 --> 13:38.116 align:middle
we're going to wait and see.

13:38.776 --> 13:39.796 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

13:39.796 --> 13:40.456 align:middle
If I could follow up.

13:40.716 --> 13:46.336 align:middle
The idea that you feel the-that
policy is restrictive suggests

13:46.336 --> 13:49.466 align:middle
that the Fed, in
general, wants to continue

13:49.466 --> 13:51.276 align:middle
to lower interest rates.

13:51.316 --> 13:54.346 align:middle
So when you look at the data
that you are dependent on,

13:54.486 --> 13:58.086 align:middle
are you looking for data that
tell you that you can cut

13:58.556 --> 14:00.836 align:middle
or data that will tell you
[that] you should hold?

14:01.046 --> 14:01.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:02.286 --> 14:05.786 align:middle
You know, we're-we're
looking-it's more the other-the

14:05.786 --> 14:08.896 align:middle
way-the way it works is,
we are looking at the data

14:08.896 --> 14:10.286 align:middle
to guide us in what
we should do.

14:10.946 --> 14:13.886 align:middle
And, you know, that,
that's what we do.

14:13.986 --> 14:17.256 align:middle
And right now, we feel like
we're in a very good place.

14:17.426 --> 14:18.546 align:middle
Policy's well positioned.

14:18.546 --> 14:21.536 align:middle
The economy's in, in quite a
good place, actually, as well.

14:22.046 --> 14:25.996 align:middle
And what, what we do expect
is to see further progress

14:25.996 --> 14:31.136 align:middle
on inflation, and, you know, as
I mentioned, as we see that-or

14:31.136 --> 14:32.816 align:middle
if we were to see
weakening in the labor market

14:32.816 --> 14:35.716 align:middle
that could foster-we could
then be in a position of,

14:35.716 --> 14:37.166 align:middle
of making further adjustments.

14:37.166 --> 14:41.206 align:middle
But, right now, we, we don't
see that, and we see things

14:41.206 --> 14:44.546 align:middle
as in a really good place for
policy and for the economy,

14:44.546 --> 14:47.536 align:middle
and so we feel like we don't
need to be in a hurry to,

14:47.696 --> 14:48.816 align:middle
to make any adjustments.

14:48.816 --> 14:49.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:50.386 --> 14:53.766 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

14:53.816 --> 14:55.436 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

14:55.606 --> 14:56.316 align:middle
Thank you very much.

14:56.316 --> 14:59.866 align:middle
In 2021, at a central bank
conference, you said, quote,

15:00.376 --> 15:03.306 align:middle
"Throughout my career in both
public and private sectors,

15:03.306 --> 15:05.816 align:middle
I've seen that the best and most
successful organizations are

15:05.816 --> 15:09.046 align:middle
often the ones that have a
strong and persistent commitment

15:09.046 --> 15:10.426 align:middle
to diversity and inclusion.

15:10.926 --> 15:13.676 align:middle
These organizations consistently
attract the best talent

15:13.676 --> 15:16.636 align:middle
by investing in and retaining
a world-class workforce."

15:16.796 --> 15:19.386 align:middle
Question, first question is,
do you still believe that?

15:19.386 --> 15:22.086 align:middle
And if so, how do you
intend to put that belief

15:22.086 --> 15:24.196 align:middle
into practice while
remaining consistent

15:24.196 --> 15:27.156 align:middle
with the recent executive
order prohibiting diversity

15:27.156 --> 15:28.096 align:middle
and inclusion efforts?

15:28.096 --> 15:28.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:28.896 --> 15:32.646 align:middle
So let me say, "yes," in
answer to your first question.

15:32.996 --> 15:35.666 align:middle
But, to the second question,
I want to say this: We are,

15:35.826 --> 15:37.486 align:middle
like others, we're
reviewing the orders

15:37.486 --> 15:40.096 align:middle
and the associated details
as they are made available.

15:40.586 --> 15:43.106 align:middle
And as has been our practice
over many Administrations,

15:43.526 --> 15:45.426 align:middle
we are working to
align our policies

15:45.426 --> 15:47.476 align:middle
with the executive
orders as appropriate

15:47.476 --> 15:49.366 align:middle
and consistent with
applicable law.

15:49.736 --> 15:51.216 align:middle
And I want to add
that I'm not going

15:51.216 --> 15:54.416 align:middle
to have anything more
specific for you today

15:54.486 --> 15:55.966 align:middle
on this whole set of issues.

15:56.256 --> 15:56.716 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:56.716 --> 15:58.556 align:middle
Well, if I could just
follow up quickly on that.

15:58.556 --> 16:01.426 align:middle
I'm wondering how you're
getting that to be consistent

16:01.426 --> 16:04.516 align:middle
with the Dodd-Frank
law's stipulations

16:04.516 --> 16:08.376 align:middle
about maintaining an Office of
Minority and Women Inclusion.

16:08.376 --> 16:08.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:08.726 --> 16:10.996 align:middle
So I did-I did mention
"consistent

16:10.996 --> 16:11.906 align:middle
with applicable law," right?

16:12.246 --> 16:13.586 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

16:13.866 --> 16:14.536 align:middle
Which is governing.

16:14.536 --> 16:15.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:17.426 --> 16:17.786 align:middle
Elizabeth.

16:18.896 --> 16:19.376 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

16:19.946 --> 16:20.566 align:middle
Thanks so much.

16:20.566 --> 16:22.046 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

16:22.766 --> 16:24.676 align:middle
Just to follow up
on Steve's question,

16:24.816 --> 16:27.526 align:middle
what reassurance can you
give the American public

16:27.566 --> 16:31.906 align:middle
that the Fed will continue to
operate independent of politics

16:31.906 --> 16:32.906 align:middle
under this Administration?

16:32.906 --> 16:34.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:34.806 --> 16:37.616 align:middle
You know, I've-as I've said
countless times over the years,

16:37.616 --> 16:39.626 align:middle
this is-this is who we
are; this is what we do.

16:39.776 --> 16:41.176 align:middle
We study the data, we,

16:41.176 --> 16:45.386 align:middle
we analyze how it will affect
the outlook and the balance

16:45.386 --> 16:47.696 align:middle
of risks, and we
use our tools to try

16:47.696 --> 16:49.696 align:middle
to give it our best
understanding,

16:49.696 --> 16:51.596 align:middle
our best thinking, try
to achieve our goals.

16:52.206 --> 16:53.736 align:middle
That's what we do;
that's always what we do.

16:53.736 --> 16:55.236 align:middle
Don't look for us
to do anything else.

16:55.546 --> 16:56.576 align:middle
And that's-you know, lots

16:56.576 --> 16:59.126 align:middle
of research shows that's
the-that's the best way

16:59.126 --> 17:00.546 align:middle
for a central bank to operate.

17:00.926 --> 17:02.636 align:middle
That'll give us the
best possible chance

17:02.636 --> 17:04.996 align:middle
to achieve these goals for the
benefit of the American people.

17:05.326 --> 17:06.766 align:middle
That's always what
we're going to do.

17:06.766 --> 17:09.066 align:middle
And people should have
confidence in that,

17:09.066 --> 17:10.356 align:middle
as I-as I said a
few minutes ago.

17:10.356 --> 17:10.916 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

17:10.916 --> 17:12.776 align:middle
You've said that the Fed
is in wait-and-see mode,

17:13.136 --> 17:15.536 align:middle
based on the policies that come
out of this Administration.

17:15.916 --> 17:19.296 align:middle
Has the Fed started
to model what policies

17:19.296 --> 17:21.886 align:middle
like mass deportations,
changes in immigration policy,

17:21.886 --> 17:23.096 align:middle
specifically, would look

17:23.096 --> 17:25.136 align:middle
like for the workforce
and for inflation?

17:25.136 --> 17:25.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:25.966 --> 17:28.936 align:middle
So one of the things our,
our staff does is they,

17:29.046 --> 17:33.026 align:middle
they look at a range of possible
outcomes, and, and they,

17:33.026 --> 17:35.676 align:middle
they tend to go from really good
to really bad, and, you know,

17:35.676 --> 17:37.666 align:middle
it's, it's one of the
best things that they do.

17:37.666 --> 17:39.306 align:middle
And in each Tealbook-you
can look

17:39.306 --> 17:40.556 align:middle
at the five-year-old Tealbooks

17:40.556 --> 17:42.726 align:middle
and see their alternative
simulations.

17:42.726 --> 17:43.496 align:middle
So that's what they do.

17:43.496 --> 17:46.296 align:middle
They, it'll be a baseline,
and then they'll show six

17:46.296 --> 17:47.916 align:middle
or seven alternative scenarios,

17:47.916 --> 17:50.366 align:middle
including really good ones
and, and not-so-good ones.

17:50.866 --> 17:55.096 align:middle
And what those do is they spark,
you know, the, the policymakers

17:55.096 --> 17:58.386 align:middle
to sort of think and understand
about the, you know, the,

17:58.386 --> 17:59.766 align:middle
the uncertainties
that surround this.

17:59.766 --> 18:03.836 align:middle
So, yes, we, we-staff does that,
and we, we're all well aware

18:03.836 --> 18:06.926 align:middle
that there are-that the range of
possibilities is always broad,

18:07.596 --> 18:10.606 align:middle
and-not just now, but always.

18:10.696 --> 18:13.246 align:middle
And you have to-it's hard
to be open to just how,

18:13.896 --> 18:17.526 align:middle
how broad the possibilities
are for an economy.

18:17.526 --> 18:19.216 align:middle
You know, no one saw
the pandemic coming,

18:19.216 --> 18:20.646 align:middle
and it was-you know,
it changed everything.

18:21.346 --> 18:24.776 align:middle
So things happen, and-but
yes, we do-we do do that.

18:25.176 --> 18:29.086 align:middle
But it's, it's one thing to
do that to make assessments

18:29.086 --> 18:31.346 align:middle
about what might happen
and begin to think

18:31.346 --> 18:34.836 align:middle
about what you might do in
that case, but you don't act

18:34.836 --> 18:37.856 align:middle
until you-until you see
much more than we see now.

18:39.286 --> 18:40.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:40.116 --> 18:41.306 align:middle
Catarina. CATARINA SARAIVA.

18:44.606 --> 18:46.586 align:middle
Catarina Saraiva,
Bloomberg News.

18:47.496 --> 18:52.256 align:middle
You know, last month you
talked about a future rate cut

18:52.526 --> 18:56.646 align:middle
as being pretty, you know,
significantly predicated

18:56.646 --> 18:58.516 align:middle
on more progress in inflation.

18:59.316 --> 19:01.746 align:middle
With the characterization
of the labor market

19:01.746 --> 19:03.776 align:middle
in the statement
today, would you say

19:03.776 --> 19:06.406 align:middle
that that's even
more so the case now?

19:07.006 --> 19:07.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:07.596 --> 19:08.506 align:middle
I'd say it's the same.

19:08.646 --> 19:10.126 align:middle
You know, we, we want
to see, you know,

19:10.486 --> 19:11.936 align:middle
further progress on inflation.

19:12.836 --> 19:15.056 align:middle
And, you know, the
story's there.

19:15.056 --> 19:19.006 align:middle
It's, it's-we're just going
to have to see the data.

19:19.006 --> 19:20.186 align:middle
At the end of the day, it,

19:20.186 --> 19:23.026 align:middle
it comes down to 12-month
inflation, because that takes

19:23.026 --> 19:25.206 align:middle
out the seasonality
issues that may exist.

19:25.826 --> 19:28.816 align:middle
And, you know, we're, we're
just going to need to see that.

19:29.336 --> 19:32.306 align:middle
We think that-we think we see
the pathway for that to happen.

19:32.306 --> 19:34.116 align:middle
One, one example-a
key example-is

19:34.116 --> 19:38.976 align:middle
that you now do see owner's
equivalent rent and, you know,

19:39.066 --> 19:41.496 align:middle
housing services-the way it's
calculated for PCE [prices],

19:41.496 --> 19:43.396 align:middle
you see that coming down
pretty steadily now,

19:43.876 --> 19:45.406 align:middle
and that's the-that's
the place where the,

19:45.406 --> 19:46.646 align:middle
most of the remaining gap is.

19:46.906 --> 19:49.616 align:middle
In addition, a big part of
the overrun, as you will know,

19:49.996 --> 19:51.546 align:middle
was [coming] from
nonmarket services [prices],

19:51.546 --> 19:53.006 align:middle
which don't tend to send much
signal [about future inflation].

19:53.006 --> 19:56.076 align:middle
So you can look through all
that and, and think, "Okay,

19:56.216 --> 19:58.746 align:middle
that then we seem to be set
up for further progress."

19:59.416 --> 20:01.796 align:middle
But being seem to set
up for it is one thing;

20:01.946 --> 20:02.866 align:middle
having it is another.

20:03.226 --> 20:04.716 align:middle
So we, we're going to
want to see further,

20:04.946 --> 20:06.266 align:middle
further progress on inflation.

20:06.936 --> 20:09.596 align:middle
Remember, we're not, you know,
we're, we're under-2 percent,

20:09.696 --> 20:11.726 align:middle
but our goal is 2
percent, and we do mean

20:11.726 --> 20:13.226 align:middle
to get back sustainably
to 2 percent.

20:14.256 --> 20:14.766 align:middle
CATARINA SARAIVA.

20:14.766 --> 20:16.636 align:middle
And, and in terms of the
labor market, I mean,

20:16.736 --> 20:19.816 align:middle
how-is that broadly-you
know, you said a broad set

20:19.816 --> 20:22.886 align:middle
of indicators show that
it's in pretty solid place.

20:22.926 --> 20:24.986 align:middle
Was there broad agreement
on that?

20:24.986 --> 20:27.666 align:middle
There's been a few
underlying indicators

20:27.666 --> 20:31.586 align:middle
that are showing perhaps some
weakness: a low hiring rate,

20:32.026 --> 20:33.236 align:middle
you know, workers reporting

20:33.236 --> 20:35.706 align:middle
that it's increasingly
difficult to find a job.

20:35.706 --> 20:37.626 align:middle
Is that of concern
to the Committee?

20:38.176 --> 20:38.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:38.396 --> 20:38.996 align:middle
So you're right.

20:38.996 --> 20:41.576 align:middle
I mean, we look at, of
course, a very broad range.

20:41.576 --> 20:44.036 align:middle
And, so, it starts with
unemployment-sorry, with, yeah,

20:44.036 --> 20:48.376 align:middle
with the unemployment rate,
employment participation, wages,

20:48.846 --> 20:52.606 align:middle
job quits-are people
quitting?-that kind of thing.

20:52.606 --> 20:54.536 align:middle
The ratio of vacancies
to unemployed.

20:54.866 --> 20:57.316 align:middle
We look at all those things,
and you, you put your finger,

20:57.316 --> 21:00.076 align:middle
though, on-it's a low-it's
a low-hiring environment.

21:00.076 --> 21:02.136 align:middle
So if you have a job,
it's all-it's all good,

21:02.616 --> 21:04.946 align:middle
but if you-if you
have to find a job,

21:05.146 --> 21:07.656 align:middle
the job-finding rate-the
hiring rates have come down.

21:07.656 --> 21:11.616 align:middle
And that's, that's more
typical of a-let's say-let's say

21:11.616 --> 21:14.006 align:middle
that the unemployment-that
the-that the labor market is

21:14.006 --> 21:15.166 align:middle
at a sustainable level.

21:15.166 --> 21:16.466 align:middle
It's not overheated anymore.

21:16.826 --> 21:18.816 align:middle
We don't think we need
it to cool off anymore.

21:18.936 --> 21:20.596 align:middle
We do watch it extremely
carefully.

21:20.596 --> 21:22.116 align:middle
It's one of our two
goal variables.

21:22.796 --> 21:26.076 align:middle
But, yeah, I, I'd say we watch
those things quite carefully.

21:26.196 --> 21:28.356 align:middle
But nonetheless, overall,

21:28.636 --> 21:31.516 align:middle
look at the aggregate
data in the labor market.

21:31.806 --> 21:33.626 align:middle
It does seem to-the
labor market does seem,

21:33.626 --> 21:36.216 align:middle
seem to be pretty stable
and broadly in balance.

21:36.326 --> 21:38.246 align:middle
When you've got a,
an unemployment rate

21:38.246 --> 21:40.576 align:middle
that is-that has been
pretty stable now for,

21:40.576 --> 21:42.276 align:middle
for a, a full half a year.

21:44.636 --> 21:45.016 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:46.606 --> 21:47.306 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

21:47.436 --> 21:47.756 align:middle
Thank you.

21:47.756 --> 21:48.756 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

21:48.756 --> 21:50.146 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

21:50.326 --> 21:52.476 align:middle
On employment, now, you
said there's a broad range

21:52.476 --> 21:55.136 align:middle
of possibilities, but last
September you said, quote,

21:55.136 --> 21:57.156 align:middle
"We understand that
there's been quite an influx

21:57.156 --> 21:59.966 align:middle
across the borders, and
that has actually been one

21:59.966 --> 22:02.786 align:middle
of things that's allowed
unemployment rate to rise."

22:03.176 --> 22:05.226 align:middle
Now that the flow over
the border has slowed

22:05.226 --> 22:06.566 align:middle
and we're seeing deportations,

22:06.856 --> 22:08.476 align:middle
how do you expect the
unemployment rate to react?

22:08.816 --> 22:09.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:09.446 --> 22:11.816 align:middle
You know, so what's
happening is that the,

22:12.076 --> 22:14.896 align:middle
the flows across the border have
decreased very significantly,

22:14.896 --> 22:17.226 align:middle
and there's every reason
to expect that to continue.

22:18.556 --> 22:24.036 align:middle
And so-but job creation has come
down a bit, too, so, you know,

22:24.606 --> 22:27.056 align:middle
if those two things come
down together, that,

22:27.336 --> 22:29.466 align:middle
that sort of can be a reason

22:29.466 --> 22:31.316 align:middle
for the unemployment
rate to stabilize.

22:31.746 --> 22:33.496 align:middle
In other words, a
breakeven rate-as,

22:33.496 --> 22:37.056 align:middle
as population growth slows, the
breakeven rate that you need

22:37.056 --> 22:41.306 align:middle
in new jobs to make-to,
to make jobs for,

22:41.726 --> 22:43.596 align:middle
for workers declines as well.

22:44.086 --> 22:46.556 align:middle
So that seems to be something
about what's happening.

22:46.556 --> 22:50.516 align:middle
You, you do see a very-a very
flat unemployment rate at a time

22:50.516 --> 22:52.146 align:middle
when you've seen significant
declines [in job growth].

22:52.526 --> 22:53.086 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

22:53.086 --> 22:54.646 align:middle
I want to ask you
about Fed employment.

22:54.646 --> 22:57.866 align:middle
I know that tax money is not
used here, but Elon Musk alleges

22:57.866 --> 23:00.316 align:middle
that the Fed is, quote,
"absurdly overstaffed."

23:00.716 --> 23:02.616 align:middle
We've seen the Executive
Branch push

23:02.826 --> 23:04.116 align:middle
to reduce the federal workforce.

23:04.116 --> 23:05.056 align:middle
I just want to get
your reaction.

23:05.506 --> 23:06.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:06.356 --> 23:08.786 align:middle
You know, we, we run a
very careful budget process

23:08.786 --> 23:10.756 align:middle
where we're fully aware
that what, that, you know,

23:10.756 --> 23:12.946 align:middle
we owe the-we owe
that to the public,

23:12.946 --> 23:13.946 align:middle
and we believe we do that.

23:14.556 --> 23:16.726 align:middle
I, I've got no further
comment than that, thanks.

23:17.766 --> 23:18.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:19.956 --> 23:21.176 align:middle
Chris. CHRIS RUGABER.

23:21.176 --> 23:23.546 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

23:23.716 --> 23:26.096 align:middle
President Trump has said
he will lower inflation

23:26.096 --> 23:28.946 align:middle
by reducing gas and
energy costs.

23:29.066 --> 23:33.126 align:middle
Do you see such costs as a
particular driver of inflation,

23:33.126 --> 23:34.926 align:middle
and would lowering them
have a dramatic effect?

23:35.166 --> 23:35.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:35.586 --> 23:37.456 align:middle
Chris, I'm not going
to-I'm not going to react

23:37.456 --> 23:38.946 align:middle
or discuss anything that,

23:38.946 --> 23:40.676 align:middle
that any elected
politician might say.

23:40.676 --> 23:41.546 align:middle
So I'll give you a mulligan.

23:41.896 --> 23:42.676 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

23:42.676 --> 23:44.316 align:middle
Okay, oh, thank you.

23:44.346 --> 23:46.626 align:middle
Well, nearly two weeks ago the
Fed said it was withdrawing

23:46.626 --> 23:49.196 align:middle
from the Network for Greening
the Financial System even as,

23:49.286 --> 23:51.946 align:middle
you know, even as we have
significant wildfires

23:51.946 --> 23:54.016 align:middle
in Los Angeles doing
billions of dollars in damage.

23:54.596 --> 23:57.286 align:middle
And, of course, the NGFS, as
you know, is a group to talk

23:57.286 --> 23:59.966 align:middle
about how the financial system
could address climate change.

24:00.906 --> 24:03.086 align:middle
Many commentators did see
the timing as political.

24:03.086 --> 24:04.996 align:middle
Why did you leave
that organization?

24:05.556 --> 24:06.506 align:middle
Can you explain that decision?

24:06.616 --> 24:06.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:06.946 --> 24:07.516 align:middle
Sure, I'd be glad to.

24:08.016 --> 24:10.456 align:middle
So we considered this, you
know, really at length,

24:10.456 --> 24:12.716 align:middle
and we did decide to
withdraw from the NGFS.

24:13.186 --> 24:15.046 align:middle
And, really, the
reason is that the,

24:15.346 --> 24:21.296 align:middle
the work that the NGFS does has
broadened very significantly.

24:21.296 --> 24:23.116 align:middle
Think about nature-related risks

24:23.116 --> 24:25.156 align:middle
and biodiversity and
things like that.

24:25.796 --> 24:30.346 align:middle
In addition, the, the work
of the NGFS is, is, in,

24:30.646 --> 24:33.866 align:middle
in significant part intended
to-and this is a quote-"mobilize

24:33.866 --> 24:36.276 align:middle
mainstream finance to support
the transition toward a

24:36.276 --> 24:37.256 align:middle
sustainable economy."

24:37.866 --> 24:39.576 align:middle
So we joined to get the benefit

24:39.576 --> 24:42.716 align:middle
of understanding what other
central banks were doing and,

24:42.786 --> 24:44.396 align:middle
and seeing research
and things like that.

24:45.076 --> 24:50.316 align:middle
I think this is just way
beyond any plausible mandate

24:50.316 --> 24:51.576 align:middle
that you could attribute
to the Fed,

24:52.166 --> 24:53.826 align:middle
and so we have a
quite narrow role,

24:53.826 --> 24:56.936 align:middle
as I've-as I've said many
times, and I think that,

24:56.936 --> 25:01.546 align:middle
that the activities of the NGFS
are not a good fit for the Fed,

25:02.136 --> 25:03.716 align:middle
given our current
mandate and authority.

25:03.716 --> 25:06.696 align:middle
So, you know, and I just think
it was time to acknowledge that.

25:07.326 --> 25:10.336 align:middle
You know, the, the process-this
process dates back-thinking

25:10.336 --> 25:12.026 align:middle
about it dates back
a couple of years.

25:12.816 --> 25:14.966 align:middle
I made the decision to
bring this to the Board,

25:15.596 --> 25:16.766 align:middle
you know, some months ago.

25:16.766 --> 25:20.686 align:middle
It just-it just-the process
just took time to get here, and,

25:20.686 --> 25:22.106 align:middle
and this is when-this
is when we,

25:22.256 --> 25:23.546 align:middle
we got in and voted on it, so.

25:23.966 --> 25:25.206 align:middle
And I'm aware of
how it can look,

25:25.206 --> 25:28.256 align:middle
but it was really not driven by
politics; it was driven by kind

25:28.256 --> 25:29.706 align:middle
of the disconnect
between the work

25:29.706 --> 25:31.636 align:middle
of the NGFS and our mandate.

25:31.806 --> 25:33.376 align:middle
Other central banks
have different mandates

25:33.806 --> 25:34.836 align:middle
and belong to the NGFS.

25:34.836 --> 25:36.496 align:middle
We have no, no criticism
of them.

25:36.496 --> 25:38.366 align:middle
But it just isn't-it's
not right for the Fed.

25:40.456 --> 25:41.356 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:43.396 --> 25:44.746 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

25:44.866 --> 25:46.596 align:middle
Thanks. Andrew Ackerman
with the Washington Post.

25:47.076 --> 25:48.466 align:middle
I'm wondering if
you could talk more

25:48.466 --> 25:50.796 align:middle
about what further progress
would look like for consumers.

25:52.226 --> 25:53.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:54.076 --> 25:58.596 align:middle
Well, 2 percent-inflation
down to-inflation

25:58.596 --> 26:00.296 align:middle
down to 2 percent
sustainably is what,

26:00.296 --> 26:01.336 align:middle
what we're trying to achieve.

26:01.336 --> 26:06.386 align:middle
You know, we're, we're somewhat
above that, as you know, and,

26:06.386 --> 26:09.466 align:middle
you know, we, we want to see,
you know, serial readings

26:09.466 --> 26:12.456 align:middle
that suggest that we're making
further progress on inflation.

26:12.456 --> 26:13.596 align:middle
That's what we want to see.

26:13.596 --> 26:16.516 align:middle
And consumers will, will
pick that up, of course,

26:16.516 --> 26:17.446 align:middle
in the things that they buy

26:17.586 --> 26:18.936 align:middle
at the grocery store
or at the store.

26:18.936 --> 26:19.886 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

26:19.886 --> 26:20.526 align:middle
The other thing I wanted

26:20.526 --> 26:24.516 align:middle
to ask was just how far away
you think you are from neutral.

26:26.126 --> 26:26.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:26.876 --> 26:29.796 align:middle
Yeah, you can't know with
any precision, of course.

26:30.466 --> 26:32.776 align:middle
As I like to say, that you know
that-you know the neutral rate

26:32.826 --> 26:33.606 align:middle
by its works.

26:34.096 --> 26:37.376 align:middle
So I think, you know,
at 4.3 percent we're,

26:37.456 --> 26:41.976 align:middle
we're above pretty much everyone
on the Committee's estimates

26:41.976 --> 26:44.816 align:middle
of the longer-run neutral.

26:44.996 --> 26:46.746 align:middle
I think our eyes are telling us

26:46.796 --> 26:49.296 align:middle
that our policy is having
the effects on the economy.

26:49.296 --> 26:50.626 align:middle
That's really the
question we ask.

26:50.626 --> 26:52.466 align:middle
You know, you can
consult models,

26:52.466 --> 26:54.206 align:middle
empirical models,
theoretical models.

26:54.916 --> 26:56.506 align:middle
You really have to just
look out the window

26:56.506 --> 27:00.856 align:middle
and see how your-how your policy
rate is affecting the economy.

27:01.146 --> 27:03.596 align:middle
And I think we see that it's
having meaningful effects

27:04.256 --> 27:05.816 align:middle
in bringing inflation
under control.

27:06.016 --> 27:09.686 align:middle
It has helped bring the labor
market into balance as well.

27:09.686 --> 27:11.246 align:middle
So that's what we think.

27:11.246 --> 27:12.956 align:middle
I, I would say we're
meaningfully above it.

27:13.036 --> 27:16.446 align:middle
I, I am-I, I have
no illusion that,

27:16.526 --> 27:19.826 align:middle
that anyone knows
precisely how much that is.

27:20.276 --> 27:21.866 align:middle
And, but it, you
know-not knowing that,

27:21.866 --> 27:26.186 align:middle
and having cut 100 basis points,
means that it's appropriate

27:26.186 --> 27:28.486 align:middle
that we-that we not
be in a hurry

27:28.736 --> 27:29.836 align:middle
to make further adjustments.

27:31.456 --> 27:32.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:33.386 --> 27:34.626 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

27:35.046 --> 27:36.196 align:middle
Victoria Guida with Politico.

27:36.326 --> 27:40.856 align:middle
So, as a general matter, when
it comes to executive orders

27:40.936 --> 27:45.946 align:middle
and OMB memos, do those always
apply to the Fed, sometimes,

27:45.946 --> 27:49.786 align:middle
never, or do you just
often voluntarily comply?

27:50.416 --> 27:51.646 align:middle
What is-what is the
legality there?

27:51.646 --> 27:51.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:51.876 --> 27:54.026 align:middle
So it's been-it's been our
practice, as I mentioned,

27:54.696 --> 27:59.046 align:middle
to work to align our policies
to those that are mentioned in,

27:59.046 --> 27:59.976 align:middle
in the executive orders.

27:59.976 --> 28:01.276 align:middle
And that's-I'm just going
to leave it at that.

28:01.276 --> 28:03.456 align:middle
As I mentioned, I'm not
going to-I'm not going

28:03.456 --> 28:06.676 align:middle
to go any deeper than that
or, or get into any deeper

28:06.676 --> 28:07.796 align:middle
into this set of issues today.

28:10.046 --> 28:12.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:12.696 --> 28:15.086 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

28:15.156 --> 28:17.096 align:middle
Claire Jones from
the Financial Times.

28:17.476 --> 28:19.316 align:middle
Two questions, if
I may, on tariffs.

28:19.966 --> 28:22.766 align:middle
Well, first of all, we've
seen global trade wars before,

28:23.346 --> 28:25.576 align:middle
notably in 2019 last
time around.

28:25.576 --> 28:28.406 align:middle
But then we were then in
a very different place

28:28.406 --> 28:30.366 align:middle
on both inflation and growth.

28:31.586 --> 28:34.256 align:middle
If we see tariffs of the
same sort of magnitude

28:34.256 --> 28:38.876 align:middle
that we got then-which I know is
a big if-what do you think might

28:39.026 --> 28:41.196 align:middle
be different this time around?

28:41.836 --> 28:45.646 align:middle
And, secondly, Tiff Macklem
said there was no doubt

28:46.216 --> 28:50.186 align:middle
that the threat of tariffs
was a big driver of the cut

28:50.186 --> 28:51.746 align:middle
by the Bank of Canada today.

28:52.196 --> 28:55.536 align:middle
What sort of information
would the Fed need to see

28:55.536 --> 29:00.106 align:middle
on tariffs before it was willing
to take such a preemptive move?

29:00.546 --> 29:00.856 align:middle
Thank you.

29:00.906 --> 29:01.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:01.156 --> 29:01.766 align:middle
Sorry, make a-?

29:01.766 --> 29:02.176 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

29:02.276 --> 29:04.406 align:middle
To-what sort of information
would you need

29:04.406 --> 29:05.396 align:middle
to see on tariffs?

29:05.396 --> 29:09.046 align:middle
Would you need to see a
strategy, actual implementation,

29:09.126 --> 29:11.686 align:middle
actual movement of
inflation expectations,

29:12.106 --> 29:14.676 align:middle
before you're actually
willing to change the path

29:14.676 --> 29:17.056 align:middle
of monetary policy
on the basis of it?

29:17.056 --> 29:17.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:17.746 --> 29:19.816 align:middle
So we just-so first
of all, thing,

29:19.856 --> 29:20.866 align:middle
things are a little
different now.

29:20.866 --> 29:22.946 align:middle
We've just come through
a high-inflation period,

29:23.526 --> 29:25.996 align:middle
and you can argue
that both ways.

29:25.996 --> 29:27.786 align:middle
You can-you can say that
companies have figured

29:27.786 --> 29:29.436 align:middle
out that they do like
to raise prices, but,

29:29.676 --> 29:32.066 align:middle
but we also hear a lot
from companies these days

29:32.106 --> 29:34.886 align:middle
that consumers have really
had it with price increases,

29:34.886 --> 29:36.716 align:middle
and so I don't know
how that shakes out.

29:36.716 --> 29:39.066 align:middle
Nonetheless, you're
coming through a situation

29:39.066 --> 29:41.036 align:middle
where we're not quite back
to 2 percent, and that,

29:41.036 --> 29:41.796 align:middle
that's just different.

29:42.146 --> 29:45.056 align:middle
In addition, you know, the
trade-the kind of footprint

29:45.056 --> 29:49.506 align:middle
of trade is, has changed a,
a lot, as trade is now spread

29:49.506 --> 29:53.406 align:middle
around the, you know-it's not as
concentrated in China as it was.

29:53.406 --> 29:54.776 align:middle
There was a lot more
manufacturing.

29:54.806 --> 29:57.256 align:middle
It moved to Mexico
and other places.

29:57.416 --> 30:00.806 align:middle
So, so there were differences,
and I just think the,

30:00.976 --> 30:03.826 align:middle
the range of possibilities
is, is very, very wide.

30:04.406 --> 30:07.156 align:middle
We just don't know, and I, I
don't want to start speculating,

30:07.256 --> 30:10.576 align:middle
as tempting as it is,
because we really don't know.

30:10.656 --> 30:16.786 align:middle
And we didn't know, by the
way, in, I guess, 2018.

30:17.036 --> 30:17.976 align:middle
Yeah, we didn't really know.

30:18.186 --> 30:20.496 align:middle
And it, you know, the-again,

30:20.496 --> 30:23.606 align:middle
the range of possibilities,
very, very wide.

30:24.446 --> 30:26.286 align:middle
We don't know what's
going to be tariff.

30:26.286 --> 30:29.206 align:middle
We don't know for how long
or how much, what countries.

30:29.456 --> 30:30.846 align:middle
We don't know about retaliation.

30:31.126 --> 30:33.016 align:middle
We don't know how
it's going to transmit

30:33.116 --> 30:35.986 align:middle
through the economy
to consumers.

30:35.986 --> 30:37.946 align:middle
That's, that really
does remain to be seen.

30:38.076 --> 30:39.716 align:middle
You know, there are
lots of places

30:39.716 --> 30:43.046 align:middle
where that-where that-where
that price increase

30:43.396 --> 30:44.566 align:middle
from the tariff can show

30:44.566 --> 30:47.846 align:middle
up between the manufacturer
and a consumer.

30:48.566 --> 30:49.976 align:middle
Just so many variables.

30:49.976 --> 30:51.286 align:middle
So we're just going to
have to wait and see.

30:51.286 --> 30:53.366 align:middle
And, you know, the best we
can do is what we've done,

30:53.366 --> 30:55.826 align:middle
which is study up on
this and, you know,

30:55.826 --> 30:58.166 align:middle
look at historical
experience, read the literature,

30:58.696 --> 31:01.416 align:middle
and think about the
factors that might matter,

31:01.946 --> 31:03.946 align:middle
and then we'll just
have to see-have

31:03.946 --> 31:05.286 align:middle
to see how it-how it goes.

31:05.466 --> 31:07.766 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:07.836 --> 31:08.156 align:middle
Courtenay.

31:08.936 --> 31:10.316 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

31:11.806 --> 31:12.416 align:middle
Thank you.

31:12.416 --> 31:14.766 align:middle
Courtenay Brown from Axios.

31:14.856 --> 31:16.616 align:middle
Two unrelated questions.

31:16.646 --> 31:20.486 align:middle
The first is whether or not
there was any discussion

31:20.486 --> 31:24.226 align:middle
about QT and the timeline for
ending QT at this meeting.

31:24.596 --> 31:27.416 align:middle
And then the second
question is just-I wonder

31:27.416 --> 31:29.246 align:middle
if the AI-prompted selloff

31:29.246 --> 31:32.136 align:middle
in the stock market this
week signaled anything to you

31:32.136 --> 31:34.256 align:middle
about the state of
financial conditions.

31:35.146 --> 31:35.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:36.426 --> 31:40.386 align:middle
So, on QT, on-let's,
let's talk about runoff.

31:40.386 --> 31:42.956 align:middle
So the most recent
data do suggest

31:42.956 --> 31:44.486 align:middle
that reserves are
still abundant.

31:45.046 --> 31:48.416 align:middle
Reserves remain roughly as high
as they were when runoff began.

31:48.796 --> 31:50.486 align:middle
And the federal funds
rate has been very steady

31:50.486 --> 31:51.506 align:middle
within the target range.

31:51.506 --> 31:56.006 align:middle
We track a, a bunch of metrics,
and they do tend to point to,

31:56.066 --> 31:58.266 align:middle
to reserves being abundant.

31:59.136 --> 32:01.266 align:middle
We do intend to reduce the
size of our balance sheet

32:01.266 --> 32:02.426 align:middle
to a level that's consistent

32:02.426 --> 32:04.386 align:middle
with implementing
monetary policy efficiently

32:04.386 --> 32:07.096 align:middle
and effectively in our
ample-reserves regime.

32:07.546 --> 32:10.006 align:middle
We're closely monitoring
a, a range of indicators

32:10.006 --> 32:13.466 align:middle
to assess conditions, and that
should provide signals whether

32:13.596 --> 32:16.356 align:middle
reserves are approaching a level
that could be judged as, quote,

32:16.356 --> 32:17.506 align:middle
"somewhat above ample."

32:18.096 --> 32:22.766 align:middle
I, I don't have anything to say
to you about particular dates;

32:23.096 --> 32:26.516 align:middle
it's just that's the process,
and what we see is, is that,

32:26.566 --> 32:28.496 align:middle
that rates do appear
to be abundant.

32:29.596 --> 32:31.706 align:middle
As always, we stand ready
to take appropriate action

32:31.706 --> 32:34.206 align:middle
to support the smooth
transition of monetary policy,

32:34.456 --> 32:36.536 align:middle
including to adjust the
details of our approach

32:36.536 --> 32:38.876 align:middle
for reducing the size of
the balance sheet in light

32:38.876 --> 32:42.036 align:middle
of economic and financial
developments.

32:42.036 --> 32:46.166 align:middle
On, on AI, it's a big event
in, in the stock market

32:46.166 --> 32:48.906 align:middle
and in particular parts
of the stock market.

32:48.906 --> 32:50.226 align:middle
I mean, what, what
really matters

32:50.226 --> 32:52.506 align:middle
for us is macro developments,

32:52.506 --> 32:55.956 align:middle
and that means substantial
changes in financial conditions

32:55.956 --> 32:57.956 align:middle
that are persistent
for a period of time.

32:57.956 --> 33:01.526 align:middle
So I wouldn't put that
label on, on these events,

33:01.526 --> 33:03.596 align:middle
although of course we're all
watching it with interest.

33:03.596 --> 33:04.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:05.776 --> 33:07.986 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

33:08.226 --> 33:10.446 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch with
the Economist, thank you.

33:11.166 --> 33:12.696 align:middle
You mentioned in your
remarks that activity

33:12.696 --> 33:14.926 align:middle
in the housing sector
seems to have stabilized.

33:15.336 --> 33:18.306 align:middle
At the same time, since your
first rate cut in September,

33:18.766 --> 33:20.136 align:middle
long-term mortgage
rates have gone

33:20.136 --> 33:22.796 align:middle
up by a full percentage
point, back above 7 percent.

33:23.196 --> 33:25.006 align:middle
I'm wondering, kind
of looking forward,

33:25.276 --> 33:27.306 align:middle
do you think-are you confident

33:27.306 --> 33:29.196 align:middle
that activity will
remain stable,

33:29.196 --> 33:30.896 align:middle
given how elevated
mortgage rates are?

33:31.136 --> 33:32.736 align:middle
How does it fit into
your broader thinking

33:32.736 --> 33:33.576 align:middle
about the economy?

33:34.266 --> 33:34.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:34.646 --> 33:38.096 align:middle
So, as you know, as
we've reduced our,

33:38.096 --> 33:39.836 align:middle
our policy rate 100
basis points,

33:39.836 --> 33:41.196 align:middle
longer rates have gone up.

33:41.836 --> 33:46.136 align:middle
Not because of expectations-not
principally

33:46.136 --> 33:48.756 align:middle
because of expectations about
our policy or about inflation;

33:48.756 --> 33:50.816 align:middle
it's more a term premium story.

33:51.186 --> 33:53.206 align:middle
So, and, you know,
it's long rates

33:53.206 --> 33:55.306 align:middle
that matter for, for housing.

33:55.306 --> 33:59.386 align:middle
So I, I don't think-I think
these higher rates are going

33:59.426 --> 34:02.686 align:middle
to-they're, they're probably
hold back housing activities

34:02.686 --> 34:04.166 align:middle
to some extent, if
they're persistent.

34:04.166 --> 34:05.546 align:middle
We'll have to see how
long they persist.

34:06.226 --> 34:10.256 align:middle
So, you know, we, we are-we
control an overnight rate.

34:10.256 --> 34:13.456 align:middle
Generally, it, it propagates
through the whole family

34:13.456 --> 34:15.486 align:middle
of asset prices,
including interest rates.

34:15.876 --> 34:17.636 align:middle
But in this particular
case, it's all happened

34:17.636 --> 34:21.006 align:middle
at a time when, for reasons
unrelated to our policy,

34:21.506 --> 34:23.036 align:middle
longer rates have moved up.

34:23.036 --> 34:23.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:23.636 --> 34:30.266 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

34:31.196 --> 34:32.066 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

34:32.066 --> 34:33.796 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

34:34.316 --> 34:36.596 align:middle
You said you want to see
further progress on inflation.

34:37.026 --> 34:39.396 align:middle
Given that households
appear to be unhappy

34:39.426 --> 34:41.216 align:middle
with the elevated
level of prices,

34:41.216 --> 34:43.656 align:middle
do you believe the
Committee should wait

34:43.696 --> 34:47.076 align:middle
until inflation has fallen back
to target to cut rates again?

34:47.076 --> 34:47.606 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:47.896 --> 34:48.836 align:middle
No, I wouldn't say that.

34:49.836 --> 34:51.846 align:middle
We, we've never said we
need to be all the way

34:51.846 --> 34:53.976 align:middle
at target to reduce rates.

34:53.976 --> 34:55.886 align:middle
At, at any time, what we're
doing is we're looking

34:55.886 --> 34:58.966 align:middle
at the economy and asking
whether our policy stance is the

34:58.966 --> 35:01.606 align:middle
right one to achieve maximum
employment and price stability.

35:02.176 --> 35:05.026 align:middle
So I, I think if-we would
want to see further progress,

35:05.596 --> 35:08.866 align:middle
but we think our-we
think our-as I mentioned,

35:08.866 --> 35:11.026 align:middle
we think that our policy
stance is restrictive.

35:11.326 --> 35:12.356 align:middle
Meaningfully restrictive.

35:12.506 --> 35:15.216 align:middle
Not highly restrictive, but
meaningfully restrictive.

35:15.686 --> 35:18.266 align:middle
And so I would think we need
to see further progress.

35:18.266 --> 35:20.926 align:middle
I wouldn't say all the
way back down to 2 percent

35:20.926 --> 35:23.666 align:middle
on a sustainable basis,
although we'd love to see that,

35:23.666 --> 35:24.456 align:middle
of course, and we will.

35:24.456 --> 35:25.226 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

35:25.226 --> 35:28.076 align:middle
And a separate question
for you, on tariffs.

35:28.076 --> 35:30.406 align:middle
Curious whether the
threat of tariffs

35:30.816 --> 35:32.676 align:middle
and not knowing whether
they could stick

35:32.676 --> 35:35.096 align:middle
or not creates uncertainty
for business here

35:35.096 --> 35:37.966 align:middle
in the United States and
could cause them to pull back,

35:37.966 --> 35:39.796 align:middle
ultimately weighing on growth.

35:40.136 --> 35:42.476 align:middle
Does the threat of
tariffs cause you

35:42.476 --> 35:44.476 align:middle
to ponder your growth forecast?

35:44.906 --> 35:45.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:46.256 --> 35:49.666 align:middle
You know, we-I want to avoid
commenting, even indirectly,

35:49.666 --> 35:51.816 align:middle
on the conduct of tariffs.

35:51.816 --> 35:54.096 align:middle
You know, it's not our job,
and it's not our job to comment

35:54.096 --> 35:55.966 align:middle
on the moves that people make.

35:55.966 --> 35:57.046 align:middle
So I, I wouldn't want

35:57.046 --> 36:00.536 align:middle
to criticize anything that's
happening or really comment

36:00.536 --> 36:02.546 align:middle
on it one way or another-praise
it, for that matter.

36:02.546 --> 36:03.756 align:middle
It's just not our job.

36:04.226 --> 36:07.866 align:middle
I do think that-you know,
we found in, in 2018,

36:08.146 --> 36:09.176 align:middle
[when] there was
a lot of work done

36:09.176 --> 36:12.066 align:middle
on trade policy uncertainty,
trade policy uncertainty,

36:12.066 --> 36:15.356 align:middle
if it's large and
persistent, can start to matter

36:15.686 --> 36:18.626 align:middle
for businesses making investment
decisions and things like that.

36:18.846 --> 36:20.766 align:middle
That's not something
I'm observing today.

36:21.306 --> 36:22.816 align:middle
It's very early days for this.

36:22.876 --> 36:27.616 align:middle
But that did-I think that
did matter in 2018-19.

36:27.876 --> 36:29.956 align:middle
And, and it's one, you know,

36:29.956 --> 36:31.166 align:middle
one of many things
we'll be watching.

36:33.366 --> 36:34.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:34.786 --> 36:35.796 align:middle
Matt Egan.

36:35.876 --> 36:36.156 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

36:36.716 --> 36:37.506 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

36:37.506 --> 36:38.526 align:middle
Matt Egan from CNN.

36:38.526 --> 36:41.136 align:middle
Following up on Courtenay's
question from earlier

36:41.136 --> 36:45.606 align:middle
about the stock market, how
concerned are you, if at all,

36:45.606 --> 36:48.916 align:middle
about potential asset bubble
brewing in financial markets?

36:49.266 --> 36:52.276 align:middle
How do relatively high
market valuations factor

36:52.276 --> 36:53.976 align:middle
into considerations

36:53.976 --> 36:56.786 align:middle
about potentially lowering
interest rates further?

36:56.786 --> 36:58.236 align:middle
Is that something that's
in the back of your mind?

36:58.236 --> 36:58.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:58.986 --> 37:01.256 align:middle
So we, we look-we look at,

37:01.256 --> 37:02.976 align:middle
from a financial
stability perspective,

37:03.196 --> 37:07.746 align:middle
at asset prices generally,
along with things like leverage

37:07.856 --> 37:10.086 align:middle
in the household sector,
leverage in the banking system,

37:10.156 --> 37:12.826 align:middle
funding risk for banks,
and things like that.

37:12.826 --> 37:16.186 align:middle
But it's just one of the four
things, asset prices are.

37:16.226 --> 37:18.576 align:middle
And, yeah, I, I'd say
they're elevated by,

37:18.576 --> 37:19.896 align:middle
by many metrics right now.

37:20.096 --> 37:22.926 align:middle
A good part of that, of course,
is this thing around tech

37:23.656 --> 37:25.466 align:middle
and AI, but we, we look at that.

37:25.886 --> 37:27.516 align:middle
But, you know, we also-we look

37:27.516 --> 37:31.086 align:middle
at how resilient the
households and, and businesses

37:31.146 --> 37:33.046 align:middle
and the financial sector
are to those things.

37:33.046 --> 37:34.366 align:middle
So we look at that mainly

37:34.366 --> 37:37.096 align:middle
from our financial stability
perspective, and we think

37:37.096 --> 37:39.126 align:middle
that there's a lot of
resilience out there.

37:39.156 --> 37:42.936 align:middle
Banks have high capital,
and households are actually,

37:42.996 --> 37:45.716 align:middle
overall-not all households,
but in, in the aggregate,

37:45.716 --> 37:47.826 align:middle
households are in pretty good
shape financially these days.

37:48.436 --> 37:50.556 align:middle
So that's how we
think about that.

37:50.676 --> 37:53.836 align:middle
I, you know, we, we also-we look
at overall financial conditions,

37:53.836 --> 37:56.956 align:middle
and you've got-you can't
just take-you can't just take

37:56.956 --> 37:57.626 align:middle
equity prices.

37:57.626 --> 37:59.426 align:middle
You've got to look at rates,
too, and that, you know,

37:59.426 --> 38:01.376 align:middle
that represents a, a tightening

38:01.376 --> 38:03.776 align:middle
in conditions, with
higher rates.

38:03.776 --> 38:07.276 align:middle
So, overall, financial
conditions are probably still

38:07.556 --> 38:09.416 align:middle
somewhat accommodative,
but it's a mixed bag.

38:10.166 --> 38:11.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:11.426 --> 38:13.716 align:middle
Richard. RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

38:14.706 --> 38:15.286 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

38:15.286 --> 38:17.216 align:middle
I'm Richard Escobedo
with CBS News.

38:17.786 --> 38:19.606 align:middle
One question for you.

38:20.006 --> 38:22.266 align:middle
This month's statement notes
that unemployment has stabilized

38:22.266 --> 38:24.286 align:middle
at a low rate and that
the labor market is solid.

38:24.286 --> 38:26.206 align:middle
You walked through some
of what's driving this,

38:26.406 --> 38:28.086 align:middle
but I wonder what risks you see

38:28.086 --> 38:29.356 align:middle
that might challenge
your assessment.

38:30.626 --> 38:31.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:31.616 --> 38:33.336 align:middle
Well, the things we watch,
we discussed earlier.

38:33.336 --> 38:35.886 align:middle
One, one is, is that
there's a low hiring rate.

38:36.326 --> 38:39.086 align:middle
And so that if, if there were
to be a spike in layoffs,

38:39.086 --> 38:42.416 align:middle
if companies were to
start to reduce headcount,

38:42.566 --> 38:44.556 align:middle
you would see unemployment
go up pretty quickly,

38:44.556 --> 38:46.206 align:middle
because the hiring
rate is quite low.

38:46.586 --> 38:48.016 align:middle
So that, that's one
thing we look at.

38:48.486 --> 38:51.196 align:middle
I think it's also-it's
worth pointing out that

38:52.476 --> 38:55.616 align:middle
for lower-income
households, they are-they're

38:55.616 --> 38:56.756 align:middle
under significant pressure.

38:57.406 --> 39:00.256 align:middle
And in the aggregate,
the numbers are good,

39:00.256 --> 39:01.866 align:middle
but we know that
people at the lower end

39:01.866 --> 39:05.706 align:middle
of the income spectrum are, are
struggling with, with costs.

39:05.706 --> 39:07.476 align:middle
And, really, it's,
it's high inflation

39:07.476 --> 39:08.606 align:middle
for the basics of life.

39:08.946 --> 39:11.596 align:middle
It's not so much the inflation
now; it's the price level,

39:11.966 --> 39:14.136 align:middle
because inflation
has raised prices.

39:14.416 --> 39:17.196 align:middle
Inflation is now
closer-much closer to target,

39:17.196 --> 39:18.526 align:middle
but people are really
feeling that.

39:19.196 --> 39:22.726 align:middle
But, you know, overall,
this is a good labor market.

39:23.026 --> 39:25.166 align:middle
Here you're at 4.1
percent unemployment.

39:26.116 --> 39:27.946 align:middle
That's, that's just
a really good level,

39:27.946 --> 39:31.186 align:middle
and you've been solidly there
now for six, seven months.

39:31.796 --> 39:34.316 align:middle
And job creation is
pretty close to a level

39:34.316 --> 39:39.316 align:middle
that will hold the unemployment
rate there, given, given that,

39:39.316 --> 39:42.196 align:middle
you know, there'll be much
slower population growth.

39:42.546 --> 39:43.236 align:middle
RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

39:43.236 --> 39:44.146 align:middle
One more question.

39:44.706 --> 39:46.796 align:middle
Some of the uncertainty
around immigration policy.

39:47.716 --> 39:50.646 align:middle
Is-in your assessment, is that
making it harder for businesses

39:50.646 --> 39:53.896 align:middle
and the Fed to plan
going forward?

39:54.526 --> 39:54.906 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:55.026 --> 39:57.286 align:middle
You know, we hear
anecdotal reports, but I,

39:57.286 --> 39:59.286 align:middle
I don't see-there's nothing
in the data yet on that.

39:59.286 --> 40:01.276 align:middle
But you do-you hear-you
hear that kind of thing

40:01.276 --> 40:02.936 align:middle
about construction, for example.

40:02.936 --> 40:06.846 align:middle
And, and, you know,
businesses that are dependent

40:06.846 --> 40:10.016 align:middle
on immigrant labor are,
are saying that it's,

40:10.296 --> 40:13.506 align:middle
it's suddenly gotten
harder to, to get people.

40:14.056 --> 40:15.296 align:middle
But I, again-I know-you
don't see

40:15.296 --> 40:17.326 align:middle
that in the aggregate
data yet, but yes, you,

40:17.326 --> 40:18.246 align:middle
you hear it anecdotally.

40:19.976 --> 40:20.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:21.486 --> 40:22.406 align:middle
Nick. NICHOLAS JASINSKI.

40:23.516 --> 40:24.236 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

40:24.236 --> 40:25.636 align:middle
Nicholas Jasinski from Barron's.

40:26.316 --> 40:30.636 align:middle
The uncertainty is certainly a
theme today, and I'm wondering,

40:30.636 --> 40:34.916 align:middle
are there any periods from
your career, or as it relates

40:34.916 --> 40:37.586 align:middle
to markets, the economy, what's
going on here in Washington

40:37.586 --> 40:39.526 align:middle
and beyond, or from
lessons from history,

40:39.526 --> 40:44.016 align:middle
that may provide some guidance
for a central banker operating

40:44.016 --> 40:45.526 align:middle
in uncertain times like today?

40:46.726 --> 40:47.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:48.786 --> 40:51.646 align:middle
I guess I'd say this:
Uncertainty is,

40:51.856 --> 40:53.226 align:middle
is with us all the time.

40:53.816 --> 40:55.906 align:middle
It's, it is human
nature, apparently,

40:55.906 --> 40:59.786 align:middle
to underestimate the-how fat
the tails are, in a way, that,

40:59.786 --> 41:02.156 align:middle
you know, the, the
possibility-we think of things

41:02.156 --> 41:03.206 align:middle
in a normal distribution,

41:03.206 --> 41:05.126 align:middle
and in the economy it's
not a normal distribution.

41:05.126 --> 41:07.816 align:middle
The tails are very fat,
meaning things can happen way

41:07.816 --> 41:08.646 align:middle
out of your expectations.

41:08.646 --> 41:09.806 align:middle
It's never not that way.

41:10.376 --> 41:13.806 align:middle
I wouldn't-you know, if you-if
you think about it-think

41:13.806 --> 41:15.556 align:middle
about the first few
months of the pandemic.

41:16.086 --> 41:17.136 align:middle
That was uncertainty.

41:17.346 --> 41:19.496 align:middle
Are we going to be able
to reopen the economy?

41:20.326 --> 41:21.046 align:middle
If so, when?

41:21.376 --> 41:21.976 align:middle
How much of it?

41:21.976 --> 41:22.816 align:middle
How long will it take?

41:23.406 --> 41:24.676 align:middle
You know, that was uncertainty.

41:25.136 --> 41:27.146 align:middle
What we have now is
a good labor market.

41:27.306 --> 41:30.576 align:middle
We have the economy growing
at, you know, 2 to 2½ percent.

41:31.016 --> 41:32.816 align:middle
Inflation's come
down to, to now the,

41:32.976 --> 41:35.266 align:middle
you know-the headline inflation
number was 2.6 [percent],

41:35.756 --> 41:37.366 align:middle
and that's what the
public experiences.

41:37.366 --> 41:38.566 align:middle
We look at-we look at core

41:38.566 --> 41:40.666 align:middle
because it's a better
indicator of future inflation.

41:41.176 --> 41:45.206 align:middle
So, yes, the price level
went up a lot for inflation,

41:45.206 --> 41:47.296 align:middle
and people are feeling that,
and, and they're not wrong.

41:47.896 --> 41:52.526 align:middle
But I-so the kind of uncertainty
we have is just a, a usual level

41:52.526 --> 41:53.646 align:middle
of uncertainty about
the economy.

41:53.646 --> 41:55.676 align:middle
But then policies-which
are, you know,

41:55.676 --> 41:57.916 align:middle
not for us to criticize
or, or praise,

41:57.916 --> 42:00.316 align:middle
really-those are-those are
policies which people are,

42:00.316 --> 42:01.696 align:middle
have been elected to implement.

42:01.906 --> 42:04.376 align:middle
They're implementing
them with a view to,

42:04.666 --> 42:05.946 align:middle
to making a better economy.

42:06.446 --> 42:10.936 align:middle
And so I, I don't think-I
wouldn't call this out as,

42:10.936 --> 42:13.196 align:middle
as a-as one of those times.

42:13.196 --> 42:15.586 align:middle
I wouldn't compare it to
the Global Financial Crisis

42:15.586 --> 42:16.356 align:middle
or anything like that,

42:16.706 --> 42:19.036 align:middle
given that we have actually a
very good economy right now.

42:19.036 --> 42:20.136 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:21.586 --> 42:25.036 align:middle
Evan. EVAN RYSER.

42:25.326 --> 42:27.486 align:middle
Evan Ryser with Market
News International.

42:27.516 --> 42:30.336 align:middle
Chair Powell, is a March
cut still on the table?

42:30.566 --> 42:32.096 align:middle
And then, additionally,
are you looking

42:32.096 --> 42:35.226 align:middle
to see better-than-expected
data on inflation to cut,

42:35.706 --> 42:38.146 align:middle
or are you looking for inflation
data that roughly aligns

42:38.146 --> 42:39.366 align:middle
with current forecasts?

42:39.576 --> 42:39.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:39.986 --> 42:43.546 align:middle
So, as, as I mentioned,
the economy's strong,

42:43.546 --> 42:45.916 align:middle
the labor market's
solid, downside risks

42:45.916 --> 42:47.466 align:middle
to the labor market
appear to have abated,

42:48.096 --> 42:50.386 align:middle
and we think disinflation
continues on a slow

42:50.386 --> 42:51.626 align:middle
and sometimes bumpy path.

42:52.476 --> 42:54.846 align:middle
That tells me and
the other members

42:54.846 --> 42:57.136 align:middle
of the Committee-the broad sense
of the Committee, actually,

42:57.136 --> 42:59.086 align:middle
is that we don't
need to be in a hurry

42:59.086 --> 43:00.556 align:middle
to adjust our policy stance.

43:00.816 --> 43:02.196 align:middle
Your second question was?

43:03.096 --> 43:04.636 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

43:04.896 --> 43:05.706 align:middle
Whether or not you need

43:05.706 --> 43:08.096 align:middle
to see better-than-expected
inflation data

43:08.206 --> 43:10.436 align:middle
or just inflation data
that roughly aligns

43:10.436 --> 43:11.776 align:middle
with your current forecasts.

43:11.826 --> 43:12.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:13.706 --> 43:15.026 align:middle
You know, it's one of
those things we'll know it

43:15.026 --> 43:15.606 align:middle
when we see it.

43:15.646 --> 43:18.086 align:middle
But more, more the-the
expectation is

43:18.086 --> 43:21.006 align:middle
that we will make
continued progress, and,

43:21.056 --> 43:24.066 align:middle
you know, that's what we want.

43:24.066 --> 43:25.386 align:middle
We, we'll know it
when we see it.

43:25.386 --> 43:27.576 align:middle
When we-it, it's going
to have to be something

43:27.576 --> 43:29.456 align:middle
that isn't just idiosyncratic.

43:29.456 --> 43:31.966 align:middle
You're going to want to see,
you know, continued progress

43:31.966 --> 43:34.696 align:middle
with housing services
inflation; you're going to want

43:34.696 --> 43:37.196 align:middle
to see inflation behaving in
a way that builds confidence

43:37.196 --> 43:39.256 align:middle
that we are really
making progress.

43:39.256 --> 43:40.346 align:middle
That's what it's going to be.

43:40.346 --> 43:44.066 align:middle
And, I mean, is, is that
better than our expectations?

43:44.066 --> 43:46.076 align:middle
If we expect to see that,
it's just a question of when.

43:46.076 --> 43:47.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:48.446 --> 43:51.366 align:middle
Scott. SCOTT HORSLEY.

43:52.046 --> 43:52.526 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

43:52.526 --> 43:53.406 align:middle
Scott Horsley from NPR.

43:53.946 --> 43:56.086 align:middle
In your five-year
review, you said the,

43:56.236 --> 43:59.546 align:middle
the 2 percent inflation
target won't be on the table.

43:59.546 --> 44:01.776 align:middle
Can you talk a little about why?

44:01.776 --> 44:04.616 align:middle
Is that because you think
that's the right target,

44:04.616 --> 44:06.706 align:middle
or is it because you don't want
to move the goalpost midgame,

44:06.706 --> 44:08.306 align:middle
or what's, what's behind that?

44:08.756 --> 44:09.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:09.306 --> 44:12.946 align:middle
I think to-I think that
goal has served us well

44:13.166 --> 44:14.286 align:middle
over a long period of time.

44:14.836 --> 44:17.006 align:middle
It's also the sort
of global standard.

44:17.836 --> 44:21.916 align:middle
I think that if, if a
central bank wanted to look

44:21.916 --> 44:24.956 align:middle
at changing that, you
wouldn't do it at a time

44:24.956 --> 44:26.356 align:middle
when you're not meeting
it anyway.

44:26.696 --> 44:28.206 align:middle
I would not look at
changing it anyway,

44:28.476 --> 44:30.806 align:middle
but I certainly wouldn't
look at, at it at a time

44:30.806 --> 44:32.036 align:middle
when you're not-when
you're not meeting it.

44:32.086 --> 44:35.656 align:middle
I mean, there's just no interest
at all in changing it, if,

44:35.656 --> 44:37.196 align:middle
if I'm being at all unclear.

44:37.566 --> 44:38.816 align:middle
We're not-we're not going

44:38.816 --> 44:41.556 align:middle
to change the inflation
goal any time soon.

44:41.556 --> 44:41.926 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

44:42.316 --> 44:43.946 align:middle
And, and five years ago,

44:43.946 --> 44:47.236 align:middle
if I can paraphrase what you
all decided, it was you're going

44:47.236 --> 44:50.876 align:middle
to not raise interest
rates preemptively to,

44:51.386 --> 44:54.106 align:middle
to head off inflation until
you see sort of the whites

44:54.106 --> 44:55.236 align:middle
of the eyes of inflation,

44:55.306 --> 44:58.746 align:middle
because the solid labor
market was so beneficial.

44:59.136 --> 45:02.306 align:middle
Have the last few years changed
your thinking about that?

45:02.486 --> 45:02.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:02.756 --> 45:07.366 align:middle
So what we really said was that
we wouldn't-we wouldn't look

45:07.366 --> 45:09.106 align:middle
at a-at a strong labor market

45:09.426 --> 45:13.026 align:middle
and raise rates unless we saw
some evidence of inflation.

45:13.576 --> 45:16.736 align:middle
So the thought was that
we'd seen really low levels

45:16.736 --> 45:18.106 align:middle
of inflation-sorry,

45:18.106 --> 45:20.546 align:middle
of unemployment-with
no sign of inflation.

45:20.856 --> 45:23.986 align:middle
So why would you preemptively
want to-want to put people

45:23.986 --> 45:29.106 align:middle
out of work in the absence of,
of any kind of-any evidence

45:29.156 --> 45:32.026 align:middle
that suggested that, that this
was not a sustainable level?

45:32.346 --> 45:33.456 align:middle
It was a way of,

45:33.456 --> 45:36.576 align:middle
of acknowledging how much
humility we have about,

45:36.576 --> 45:39.236 align:middle
about the starred
variables, especially u*,

45:39.346 --> 45:41.206 align:middle
the natural rate
of unemployment.

45:41.636 --> 45:43.816 align:middle
So, that was a-that,
that was an insight.

45:43.816 --> 45:45.126 align:middle
We'll, we'll discuss that again.

45:45.126 --> 45:46.766 align:middle
That'll be one of the many
things that we discuss.

45:46.766 --> 45:49.196 align:middle
But I don't think
that insight is wrong.

45:49.626 --> 45:52.736 align:middle
We didn't-you know, we, we-what
we said was that, you know,

45:52.936 --> 45:56.426 align:middle
in-at times when inflation
persistently undershot 2

45:56.426 --> 46:01.546 align:middle
percent, we, we would
likely allow inflation

46:01.546 --> 46:04.806 align:middle
to run moderately above
2 percent for some time.

46:04.896 --> 46:05.626 align:middle
That's what we said.

46:06.576 --> 46:08.356 align:middle
That was-turned out
not to be relevant

46:08.356 --> 46:09.316 align:middle
to what actually happened.

46:09.746 --> 46:11.786 align:middle
There was nothing moderate
about the overshoot.

46:12.366 --> 46:14.356 align:middle
It was-it was an
exogenous event.

46:14.356 --> 46:16.416 align:middle
It was the pandemic, and
it happened, and, you know,

46:16.736 --> 46:20.946 align:middle
our framework permitted us
to act quite vigorously,

46:20.946 --> 46:23.746 align:middle
and we did once we decided
that that's what we should do.

46:23.746 --> 46:27.116 align:middle
Our framework had really nothing
to do with the decision to-we,

46:27.116 --> 46:29.756 align:middle
we looked at the
inflation as, as transitory,

46:30.496 --> 46:32.816 align:middle
and-right up to the point where
the data turned against that,

46:32.816 --> 46:35.096 align:middle
and when the data turned
against that in late '21,

46:35.096 --> 46:37.896 align:middle
we changed our, our view,
and we raised rates a lot,

46:37.896 --> 46:40.656 align:middle
and here we are at 4.1
percent unemployment

46:40.656 --> 46:42.196 align:middle
and inflation way down.

46:42.756 --> 46:47.036 align:middle
But the framework was, was more
irrelevant than anything else.

46:47.036 --> 46:50.806 align:middle
The, the, that part of it-that
part of it was irrelevant.

46:50.806 --> 46:55.076 align:middle
The rest of the framework worked
just fine as, as we used it,

46:55.346 --> 46:57.546 align:middle
as it supported what we did
to bring inflation down.

46:59.256 --> 46:59.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:59.536 --> 47:00.826 align:middle
Go to Mark for the
last question.

47:02.436 --> 47:03.206 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

47:03.346 --> 47:04.086 align:middle
Hello, Chairman Powell.

47:04.086 --> 47:05.746 align:middle
Mark Hamrick with Bankrate.

47:06.626 --> 47:08.016 align:middle
As you know, in the
annual report

47:08.066 --> 47:11.016 align:middle
from the Financial
Stability Oversight Council,

47:11.016 --> 47:13.416 align:middle
among the risks outlined
is cryptocurrency.

47:13.926 --> 47:16.126 align:middle
Could you talk about
those risks now?

47:16.666 --> 47:20.196 align:middle
And regarding individuals and
households, perhaps distinct

47:20.196 --> 47:22.966 align:middle
from the concern about the
financial system, do you worry

47:23.426 --> 47:26.866 align:middle
that speculation in this
unregulated asset class could

47:26.866 --> 47:30.186 align:middle
hurt their financial well-being,
or do you think it has a place

47:30.806 --> 47:32.716 align:middle
in a household's portfolio?

47:33.286 --> 47:33.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:33.656 --> 47:36.396 align:middle
You know, so our, our
role with Bitcoin, really,

47:36.396 --> 47:40.676 align:middle
is to look at-with crypto,
really, is to look at the banks,

47:41.226 --> 47:44.986 align:middle
and, and, you know, we,
we think it's-you know,

47:45.086 --> 47:49.026 align:middle
banks are perfectly able
to serve crypto customers,

47:49.026 --> 47:51.186 align:middle
as long as they understand
and can manage the risks

47:51.186 --> 47:52.786 align:middle
and its safe, safe
and soundness.

47:52.786 --> 47:55.936 align:middle
Many of our-a good number of
our banks that we regulate

47:55.936 --> 47:57.696 align:middle
and supervise do that.

47:58.306 --> 48:00.746 align:middle
You know, the threshold
has been a little higher

48:00.746 --> 48:03.196 align:middle
for banks engaging
in crypto activities,

48:03.646 --> 48:05.876 align:middle
and that's because they're
so new, and, you know,

48:05.876 --> 48:07.796 align:middle
we don't want to make the
mistake you're-if you-if you're

48:07.796 --> 48:11.586 align:middle
making a choice to conduct
that activity inside a bank

48:11.736 --> 48:13.486 align:middle
which is inside the
federal safety net,

48:13.886 --> 48:17.596 align:middle
with deposit insurance, then
you want to be pretty sure that,

48:17.646 --> 48:19.496 align:middle
that it's a safe
and sound activity.

48:19.496 --> 48:22.586 align:middle
So, you know, we're, we're
not against innovation,

48:22.586 --> 48:26.416 align:middle
and we certainly don't
want to, to take actions

48:26.416 --> 48:30.296 align:middle
that would cause banks to, you
know, to terminate customers

48:30.296 --> 48:32.946 align:middle
who are perfectly
legal just because of,

48:33.286 --> 48:35.796 align:middle
of excess risk aversion
maybe related

48:35.796 --> 48:39.386 align:middle
to regulation and
supervision, so.

48:39.386 --> 48:40.426 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

48:40.426 --> 48:41.586 align:middle
And with respect to households

48:41.586 --> 48:43.436 align:middle
and their inclusion
in that asset class?

48:43.516 --> 48:43.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:43.936 --> 48:45.266 align:middle
I, you know, that's, that's kind

48:45.266 --> 48:48.156 align:middle
of a-it's not really
our, our bailiwick.

48:48.156 --> 48:50.066 align:middle
You want people to
be knowledgeable

48:50.066 --> 48:54.066 align:middle
about the financial
engagements that they have,

48:54.496 --> 48:55.906 align:middle
and that's why we
have, you know,

48:55.906 --> 48:57.586 align:middle
the securities law,
laws that we have.

48:57.586 --> 48:59.896 align:middle
It's why, you know, if you
read a mutual fund prospectus

48:59.896 --> 49:03.436 align:middle
or individual stock prospectus,
you, you want households

49:03.436 --> 49:06.256 align:middle
to have the chance to understand
the risk that they're taking.

49:06.256 --> 49:09.266 align:middle
And, you know, I, I do
think it would be helpful

49:09.266 --> 49:12.156 align:middle
if there were a greater
regulatory apparatus

49:12.236 --> 49:14.566 align:middle
around crypto, and I think that,

49:14.696 --> 49:17.166 align:middle
that's something Congress
was working on quite a lot.

49:17.166 --> 49:20.136 align:middle
We've actually spent a lot of
time, you know, with members

49:20.136 --> 49:23.186 align:middle
of Congress working together
with them on, on various things,

49:23.186 --> 49:25.546 align:middle
and I, I think that would be
a very constructive thing for,

49:25.756 --> 49:38.526 align:middle
for Congress to do.

49:38.526 --> 49:41.986 align:middle
Thank you.

